00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:14.490
The United States and its entire history can
be largely explained by its geography.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:20.220
Since its inception, the fundamental factor
influencing and guiding the country’s development

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.790
is that it is far from everything else.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.960
The US is bounded to the east and west by
ocean and to the north and south by friendly

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.090
and militarily weak countries and this has
meant that the US has almost never been threatened

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.420
on the home-front.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.560
Many suspect that this is why the US has such
a defined government and political structure—because

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.220
it had the time to develop the system while
it wasn’t worrying about potential invasion.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:47.370
But geography had a much deeper role in the
formation of the United States that stated

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.810
back before the states were even United.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.440
It all started back in the colonial era.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.950
The 13 colonies were essentially split into
three geographically distinct groups that

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.420
can explain why the areas look the way they
do today.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:04.420
The New England colonies were heavily forested
and had some fantastic natural ports like

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:05.869
Boston and Newport.

00:01:05.869 --> 00:01:10.149
However, the area had a short growing season
and a rocky landscape which meant that crops

00:01:10.149 --> 00:01:11.810
never grew very well.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:16.189
Therefore, the area ended up as a center for
shipbuilding activity and fishing.

00:01:16.189 --> 00:01:20.990
To get their food they would trade with the
breadbasket of America—the Middle Colonies.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.979
This mid-Atlantic region was fantastic for
farming.

00:01:23.979 --> 00:01:28.340
The area has a long, warm growing season and
plentiful, arable soil.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.999
It also has navigable rivers stretching far
inland that could help transport goods.

00:01:33.999 --> 00:01:38.090
Philadelphia and New York were the two major
cities of the colonial era in this area and

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:39.979
their locations reflects their roles.

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:45.399
You’ll notice that both New York and Philadelphia
are at the mouth of significant, navigable

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:47.939
rivers while Boston is not.

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:52.270
The inland mid-Atlantic farmers transported
their goods via these rivers so the major

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.429
cities grew as trading posts at the end of
these rivers.

00:01:55.429 --> 00:02:00.100
If New England had been a farming based economy,
it’s largest city would likely have developed

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.630
here, at the mouth of the Connecticut River,
but, even though it was navigable, the river

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.560
was of little use to the area.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.260
Having a major ocean-going port in Boston
was what was important for the maritime based

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:12.540
New England economy.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.780
Further south were the Southern Colonies—home
to some of the oldest colonial settlements.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:21.819
Now, there’s this one line that you can
put on a map to explain why southern cities

00:02:21.819 --> 00:02:23.019
are where they are.

00:02:23.019 --> 00:02:24.560
It’s called the fall line.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.290
The technical explanation is that this is
where the soft sedimentary rock that forms

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:32.760
the coastal plains meets the hard basement
rock that forms the land farther inland.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.030
The simpler explanation is, however, that
above this line rivers will have waterfalls

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.030
and rapids.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.680
Below, they’ll be flat and navigable.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:47.349
Baltimore, Richmond, Durham, Raleigh, Columbia,
Augusta, and a bunch of smaller cities all

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:50.659
sit almost exactly on the fall line.

00:02:50.659 --> 00:02:52.330
There are two reasons for this.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:56.319
The obvious one is that below these cities
the rivers were navigable so colonists could

00:02:56.319 --> 00:03:00.560
get to the cities, but what was perhaps more
important was that they were far inland which

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:01.870
gave protection.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:06.530
Spain controlled Florida for much of colonial
history and had a significant military presence.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:10.719
At the time it was reasonable to think that
they would continue their expansion by moving

00:03:10.719 --> 00:03:16.269
up the coast so colonial cities in the South
chose their locations in part based on natural

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:17.269
fortifications.

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:23.760
But some of the most significant events in
US history happened because of one fact—the

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.349
south is hot.

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:29.030
Very early into colonial history settlers
in the south figured out that the area was

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:33.409
fantastic for tobacco cultivation and England
loved tobacco.

00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:38.280
The only problem was, tobacco is a much more
labor intensive crop than wheat or beans or

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.280
corn.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.790
So the farmers bought in help… just not
voluntarily.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:47.019
The American south became one of the biggest
destinations of the slave trade and plantation

00:03:47.019 --> 00:03:49.129
owners were getting filthy rich.

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:54.170
A big reason slavery existed for so long in
the US was actually because the south was

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:55.499
so physically big.

00:03:55.499 --> 00:04:00.269
You see, the job of supreme court justice
used to be part-time.

00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:04.499
In the 19th century the justices would only
come to DC for a portion of the year and,

00:04:04.499 --> 00:04:09.130
for the rest of the year they would travel
around an assigned area and hear cases alongside

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.310
local judges.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:15.450
The problem was, the north was much more densely
populated and had better travel infrastructure

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:16.670
than the south.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:21.580
The south was vast, sparsely populated, and
had a very rudimentary road system.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:26.780
Therefore, judges assigned to the north were
given a smaller area with a larger population

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:31.200
while the southern judges were given a larger
area with a smaller population so they could

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.890
get around their whole circuit in the same
time.

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:39.640
Therefore, in the 19th century when considering
the constitutionality of slavery, the south,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:43.940
the slave owning area, was overrepresented
in the supreme court.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:49.920
The crucial Dred Scott case of 1857 which
cemented the legality of slavery in US territory

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:56.100
was decided by a bench of five southern justices
and four northern ones even though only 8

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.760
million of the country’s 27 million free
inhabitants lived in southern states.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:05.380
Although some may call it a stretch, you can
at least partially attribute the civil war

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:10.000
to the weather in the south that made tobacco
and cotton cultivation possible.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.820
So how did the US end up becoming the superpower
it is today?

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:19.390
After the colonial era the United States had
to push out west to gain the strategic advantage

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:20.390
of isolation.

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:26.190
In 1800, what is now the US was essentially
divided into three sections ruled by three

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:29.990
countries: the United States, France, and
Spain.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:32.780
In the colonial era nobody really ventured
west of the Appalachians.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:36.680
There was no real reason to and it was the
unknown.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:42.150
In fact, it wasn’t until 1789 that the first
explorer crossed the continent to the Pacific

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:43.240
overland.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:48.130
The first problem preventing expansion was
Spain and France’s ownership of the Mississippi

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:51.160
River region, or more specifically, New Orleans.

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:55.570
You see, New Orleans was perhaps the most
important city on the continent because it

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:59.470
lay where the most important river on the
continent met the ocean.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:05.700
The Mississippi River has an absolutely enormous
watershed that all drains here.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:09.040
He who controlled New Orleans controlled the
entire middle country.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:13.810
There were almost no roads going from east
to west in North America in the early 1800s

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:18.680
so to get something from Pittsburgh to New
York, it was actually easier to ship by boat

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:24.140
on the Ohio and Mississippi rivers to the
Atlantic than to make the 300 mile overland

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:25.140
trek.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.720
Rivers were how people got things around so
without access to New Orleans the US had no

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:34.300
access to the rivers and without access to
the rivers the US couldn’t get crops and

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.140
other goods from this region to the outside
world.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:41.290
Luckily, France agreed to sell New Orleans
and the rest of their huge swath of land to

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:46.690
the US in 1803 for the equivalent of $250
million 2017 dollars.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:52.360
This proved to be one of the best purchases
in history as the area now has an estimated

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:55.660
value of $1.2 trillion.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:07:01.990
The US then got access to the Pacific in 1819
in the Pacific Northwest then fully in 1848

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:06.110
at the end of the Mexican American war upon
the secession of California and the southwest

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:07.250
from Mexico.

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:10.780
The United States now existed from sea to
shining sea.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:16.910
It was at this moment when people were convinced,
with merit, that the US was untouchable.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:21.360
No real superpower existed in the western
hemisphere which meant that nobody could truly

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.930
threaten the existence of the United States.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:28.570
Any invading army would have to run supply
chains across oceans which greatly reduces

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:33.480
power and, at least historically, there was
no threat of another superpower arising on

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:37.800
the North America continent because of, once
again, geography.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:42.660
Mexico was largely a desert and had no solid
river system like the US to facilitate ease

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:43.750
of movement.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:47.830
Without a solid transport system and ability
to easily grow crops it had little chance

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:52.450
of gaining the population and level of development
necessary to rival the US.

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:55.430
Canada also had its own problems.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:59.890
It didn't have any rivers crossing the country,
it’s climate didn’t allow for much farming,

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.710
and mountainous terrain made roads difficult.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:08.930
Few realize that even today only one two-lane
road connects the entirety of eastern Canada

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:11.640
to the entirety of western Canada.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:17.330
Just last year a bridge on the Trans-Canada
highway collapsed and so, for 17 hours, there

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:22.410
was no way to drive from east to west and
the country was split in two.

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:27.190
If ever Canada was invaded by land there would
be no solid way to facilitate troop movement

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:31.330
and it would likely operate more as regions
than a cohesive country.

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:36.039
The US is therefore in a privileged position
today where thousands of miles separates it

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:38.150
from the serious issues of abroad.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:43.060
The North American continent is largely devoid
of international conflict and so the United

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:47.680
States’ success can be accurately attributed
to its geographic isolation.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:51.650
When you don’t have to worry about protecting
against a potential invasion you can devote

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:56.030
more time to education, development, science,
and politics.

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:58.800
The United States is a country that worked
almost by chance.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.150
It just happened that the right mix of people
landed in the right place to create a world

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:08.900
superpower so, without the fantastic geography
of the continent, the United States almost

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:13.800
certainly would not be the superpower it is
today.

